{
  "_about": "The sandbox's ships (Brian, 19 November 2026: 'a sandbox to play and make your own history'; design paper https://claude.ai/artifact/LV5ZA8vXKtyuDvjRbazxhc). Read only with the sandbox switched on (g.flags.sandbox, classes.ts); with it off nothing here is used. The ship classes themselves are rows of classes.json ('of' a ship kind). 'aaMost' is the most a fleet's anti-aircraft ships cut its losses to an air strike. 'styles' is how each navy's computer staff leans when it may build a class of its own (history.json stops 'whatif'), and 'likes' the classes each style orders, best first. 'refits' are the changes a ship in harbour can be given at a yard of her kind's size: 'months' out of service, 'share' of a new ship's price, 'needs' a technology, 'kinds' the ships it fits; 'mul' multiplies her numbers, 'add' adds to them, 'to' rebuilds her as another class (its row, or for a cruiser hull 'light' the numbers given), and 'once' means she can have it only once. 'board' is the drawing board: six dials on one card, each step with what it takes of the hull's room and what it costs and gives (armour and speed take a share of the hull's room and price, a flight deck 'roomShare' and 'costShare'; a small hull is ordered four at a time, a flotilla); 'value' is the most a design may be worth for its price against the standard ship of its kind (1.1: a tenth better), and above that its price is raised until it is not. 'sub' is a submarine's own small card. 'names' says how new ships are named: capital ships and carriers after the nation's home provinces, cruisers after its cities, flotillas by number.",
